WEBVTT

00:00.740 --> 00:03.040
What we're going to
prove in this video

00:03.040 --> 00:05.300
is a couple of fairly
straightforward

00:05.300 --> 00:06.837
parallelogram-related proofs.

00:06.837 --> 00:08.670
And this first one,
we're going to say, hey,

00:08.670 --> 00:10.720
if we have this
parallelogram ABCD,

00:10.720 --> 00:13.920
let's prove that the opposite
sides have the same length.

00:13.920 --> 00:19.820
So prove that AB is equal to
DC and that AD is equal to BC.

00:19.820 --> 00:21.630
So let me draw a diagonal here.

00:24.740 --> 00:26.850
And this diagonal, depending
on how you view it,

00:26.850 --> 00:29.900
is intersecting two
sets of parallel lines.

00:29.900 --> 00:32.150
So you could also consider
it to be a transversal.

00:32.150 --> 00:34.441
Actually, let me draw it a
little bit neater than that.

00:34.441 --> 00:35.790
I can do a better job.

00:35.790 --> 00:36.290
Nope.

00:36.290 --> 00:38.550
That's not any better.

00:38.550 --> 00:41.530
That is about as
good as I can do.

00:41.530 --> 00:44.000
So if we view DB,
this diagonal DB--

00:44.000 --> 00:47.780
we can view it as a transversal
for the parallel lines AB

00:47.780 --> 00:49.110
and DC.

00:49.110 --> 00:51.800
And if you view it that
way, you can pick out

00:51.800 --> 00:56.484
that angle ABD is going to
be congruent-- so angle ABD.

00:56.484 --> 00:57.900
That's that angle
right there-- is

00:57.900 --> 01:00.960
going to be congruent
to angle BDC,

01:00.960 --> 01:03.430
because they are
alternate interior angles.

01:03.430 --> 01:05.300
You have a transversal--
parallel lines.

01:05.300 --> 01:09.810
So we know that
angle ABD is going

01:09.810 --> 01:12.007
to be congruent to angle BDC.

01:16.020 --> 01:18.360
Now, you could also
view this diagonal,

01:18.360 --> 01:21.490
DB-- you could view it as
a transversal of these two

01:21.490 --> 01:26.030
parallel lines, of the other
pair of parallel lines, AD

01:26.030 --> 01:27.600
and BC.

01:27.600 --> 01:29.990
And if you look at it that
way, then you immediately

01:29.990 --> 01:38.130
see that angle DBC
right over here

01:38.130 --> 01:40.490
is going to be
congruent to angle

01:40.490 --> 01:47.690
ADB for the exact same reason.

01:47.690 --> 01:49.630
They are alternate
interior angles

01:49.630 --> 01:53.170
of a transversal intersecting
these two parallel lines.

01:53.170 --> 01:54.360
So I could write this.

01:54.360 --> 02:00.810
This is alternate
interior angles

02:00.810 --> 02:04.730
are congruent when you have
a transversal intersecting

02:04.730 --> 02:06.880
two parallel lines.

02:06.880 --> 02:09.520
And we also see that
both of these triangles,

02:09.520 --> 02:15.910
triangle ADB and triangle CDB,
both share this side over here.

02:15.910 --> 02:18.440
It's obviously equal to itself.

02:18.440 --> 02:20.425
Now, why is this useful?

02:20.425 --> 02:22.050
Well, you might
realize that we've just

02:22.050 --> 02:23.820
shown that both of
these triangles,

02:23.820 --> 02:25.340
they have this pink angle.

02:25.340 --> 02:27.532
Then they have this
side in common.

02:27.532 --> 02:28.990
And then they have
the green angle.

02:28.990 --> 02:32.580
Pink angle, side in common,
and then the green angle.

02:32.580 --> 02:35.470
So we've just shown
by angle-side-angle

02:35.470 --> 02:38.040
that these two
triangles are congruent.

02:38.040 --> 02:39.440
So let me write this down.

02:39.440 --> 02:42.320
We have shown that
triangle-- I'll

02:42.320 --> 02:47.650
go from non-labeled
to pink to green-- ADB

02:47.650 --> 02:53.105
is congruent to triangle--
non-labeled to pink to green--

02:53.105 --> 02:53.605
CBD.

02:58.810 --> 03:02.440
And this comes out of
angle-side-angle congruency.

03:09.390 --> 03:11.190
Well, what does that do for us?

03:11.190 --> 03:13.360
Well, if two triangles
are congruent, then

03:13.360 --> 03:16.350
all of the corresponding
features of the two triangles

03:16.350 --> 03:18.010
are going to be congruent.

03:18.010 --> 03:26.360
In particular, side DC
on this bottom triangle

03:26.360 --> 03:29.110
corresponds to side BA
on that top triangle.

03:29.110 --> 03:32.620
So they need to be congruent.

03:32.620 --> 03:38.230
So we get DC is going
to be equal to BA.

03:38.230 --> 03:43.370
And that's because they
are corresponding sides

03:43.370 --> 03:47.200
of congruent triangles.

03:47.200 --> 03:49.560
So this is going to
be equal to that.

03:49.560 --> 03:54.190
And by that exact same
logic, AD corresponds to CB.

03:58.260 --> 04:00.770
AD is equal to CB.

04:00.770 --> 04:03.680
And for the exact same
reason-- corresponding sides

04:03.680 --> 04:05.240
of congruent triangles.

04:05.240 --> 04:06.740
And then we're done.

04:06.740 --> 04:09.896
We've proven that opposite
sides are congruent.

04:09.896 --> 04:11.020
Now let's go the other way.

04:13.640 --> 04:16.269
Let's say that we have some
type of a quadrilateral,

04:16.269 --> 04:18.829
and we know that the
opposite sides are congruent.

04:18.829 --> 04:22.460
Can we prove to ourselves
that this is a parallelogram?

04:22.460 --> 04:24.900
Well, it's kind of the
same proof in reverse.

04:24.900 --> 04:27.080
So let's draw a
diagonal here, since we

04:27.080 --> 04:29.250
know a lot about triangles.

04:29.250 --> 04:31.760
So let me draw.

04:31.760 --> 04:34.200
There we go.

04:34.200 --> 04:35.336
That's the hardest part.

04:35.336 --> 04:36.410
Draw it.

04:36.410 --> 04:37.930
That's pretty good.

04:37.930 --> 04:38.430
All right.

04:38.430 --> 04:42.710
So we obviously know that CB
is going to be equal to itself.

04:42.710 --> 04:44.600
So I'll draw it like that.

04:44.600 --> 04:46.695
Obviously, because
it's the same line.

04:46.695 --> 04:48.320
And then we have
something interesting.

04:48.320 --> 04:51.500
We've split this quadrilateral
into two triangles, triangle

04:51.500 --> 04:56.500
ACB and triangle DBC.

04:56.500 --> 05:00.430
And notice, all three sides
of these two triangles

05:00.430 --> 05:01.650
are equal to each other.

05:01.650 --> 05:05.110
So we know by side-side-side
that they are congruent.

05:05.110 --> 05:11.125
So we know that triangle
A-- and we're starting at A,

05:11.125 --> 05:13.380
and then I'm going
to the one-hash side.

05:13.380 --> 05:20.263
So ACB is congruent
to triangle DBC.

05:24.030 --> 05:30.670
And this is by
side-side-side congruency.

05:30.670 --> 05:32.300
Well, what does that do for us?

05:32.300 --> 05:34.550
Well, it tells us that all
of the corresponding angles

05:34.550 --> 05:36.430
are going to be congruent.

05:36.430 --> 05:42.200
So for example, angle ABC
is going to be-- so let

05:42.200 --> 05:43.830
me mark that.

05:49.160 --> 05:51.780
You can say ABC is going
to be congruent to DCB.

05:57.480 --> 06:04.300
And you could say, by
corresponding angles congruent

06:04.300 --> 06:06.560
of congruent triangles.

06:06.560 --> 06:09.200
I'm just using some shorthand
here to save some time.

06:09.200 --> 06:12.080
So ABC is going to
be congruent to DCB,

06:12.080 --> 06:15.540
so these two angles are
going to be congruent.

06:15.540 --> 06:18.360
Well, this is interesting,
because here you have a line.

06:18.360 --> 06:21.460
And it's intersecting AB and CD.

06:21.460 --> 06:23.970
And we clearly see
that these things that

06:23.970 --> 06:27.720
could be alternate interior
angles are congruent.

06:27.720 --> 06:30.360
And because we have these
congruent alternate interior

06:30.360 --> 06:34.200
angles, we know that AB
must be parallel to CD.

06:34.200 --> 06:36.710
So this must be
parallel to that.

06:36.710 --> 06:41.630
So we know that AB
is parallel to CD

06:41.630 --> 06:50.350
by alternate interior angles
of a transversal intersecting

06:50.350 --> 06:51.730
parallel lines.

06:51.730 --> 06:53.840
Now, we can use that
exact same logic.

06:53.840 --> 06:56.720
We also know that angle--
let me get this right.

06:56.720 --> 07:03.785
Angle ACB is congruent
to angle DBC.

07:09.710 --> 07:16.170
And we know that by
corresponding angles congruent

07:16.170 --> 07:18.790
of congruent triangles.

07:18.790 --> 07:22.580
So we're just saying this
angle is equal to that angle.

07:22.580 --> 07:25.052
Well, once again, these could
be alternate interior angles.

07:25.052 --> 07:26.260
They look like they could be.

07:26.260 --> 07:27.280
This is a transversal.

07:27.280 --> 07:28.980
And here's two lines
here, which we're not sure

07:28.980 --> 07:30.100
whether they're parallel.

07:30.100 --> 07:33.030
But because the alternate
interior angles are congruent,

07:33.030 --> 07:34.990
we know that they are parallel.

07:34.990 --> 07:36.860
So this is parallel to that.

07:36.860 --> 07:41.500
So we know that AC
is parallel to BD

07:41.500 --> 07:43.960
by alternate interior angles.

07:48.660 --> 07:49.610
And we're done.

07:49.610 --> 07:51.390
So what we've done
is-- it's interesting.

07:51.390 --> 07:55.680
We've shown if you
have a parallelogram,

07:55.680 --> 07:57.640
opposite sides have
the same length.

07:57.640 --> 07:59.670
And if opposite sides
have the same length,

07:59.670 --> 08:01.030
then you have a parallelogram.

08:01.030 --> 08:03.340
And so we've actually proven
it in both directions.

08:03.340 --> 08:06.080
And so we can actually make what
you call an "if and only if"

08:06.080 --> 08:06.580
statement.

08:12.150 --> 08:16.020
You could say opposite sides of
a quadrilateral are parallel if

08:16.020 --> 08:18.520
and only if their
lengths are equal.

08:18.520 --> 08:20.075
And you say if and only if.

08:20.075 --> 08:21.624
So if they are
parallel, then you

08:21.624 --> 08:23.040
could say their
lengths are equal.

08:23.040 --> 08:26.410
And only if their lengths
are equal are they parallel.

08:26.410 --> 08:28.875
We've proven it in
both directions.
